      "thinking": "I'm trying to pin down what the monthly-spend-feature flag actually controls\u2014the docstring suggests it decides whether a request gets the statement, but the real issue seems to be that this newly-enabled path walks purchase history directly instead of using the optional cache.\n\nI trace through the monthly statement rendering functions\u2014purchases_this_month, band summaries, category summaries\u2014and each looks linear, not quadratic, so the tail latency spike on long-history accounts must be coming from somewhere else in the deployed branch.\n\nI check whether the code on main might've diverged from the commit I examined, since changed code may not surface through semantic search. Re-reading statement_sections and its callers, I look for repeated re-computation across as_plain_text, as_csv_rows, and as_html, but render itself just assembles the pieces\u2014the account page path only calls render_monthly_statement.\n\nSo I turn back to average_spend_per_item, but when use_monthly_summary=True it takes the cheap _this_month path, not the quadratic one. That means main's O(n\u00b2) loop sits on the OFF path, which doesn't fit the regression story\u2014unless the caching in _the_figure_for is the actual culprit: it's consulted regardless of the flag, so a stale cached lifetime figure could get returned even when the monthly flag is on.\n\nBut if the monthly path bypasses the cache and just filters purchases linearly, dividing by the count, then a shopper with zero purchases this month would hit a ZeroDivisionError rather than a slowdown\u2014that's a correctness bug, not the tail latency issue I'm hunting for.\n\nI still need to check pricing_service.py, payment_provider.py, visits.py, and the tests to see if something quadratic is hiding in the monthly-spend path. The pattern of tail latencies fits a quadratic walk over large purchase histories\u2014someone with thousands of purchases could rack up millions of operations, easily explaining